{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791591428,"nonce":"tIpyKrOONZI25aKCY/LQi2lAHHE=","message":"Correction (user, 2026-10-10): the theme is base-ui.com inspired, for Blinc's own wiki and demos, and not for external consumption. The warm paper, teal and ember palette in the description above is replaced; the squircle shape stays, now from the user's request to enforce it through the shape tokens.\n\nWhere the values come from\n- base-ui.com's published tokens, read from its stylesheet: the gray scale (surfaces #fcfcfc and #f9f9f9, contours, text #2e2e2e and #767676; dark #131313, #1a1a1a, #e6e6e6, #b3b3b3), the alpha borders, the blue, red and green in oklch, the layered shadows (none in dark, where a border separates), and the radius steps. Light values were also checked against pixels sampled from the rendered page.\n- Its look is monochrome: ink is the accent (a black checked box, a 2px ink focus outline), colour is for links, code and status.\n- Derived by me, not from the site: amber for warning, the success, warning and error tints, and the dark tints for them. Each is checked by contrast.\n- Shape follows ashui's hybrid theme: smoothing 0.4, exponent 3.3, threshold 12, with the default radius at the threshold so unmarked surfaces are squircles. The control radius stays at Base UI's 6px (md), below the threshold, so controls stay circular.\n- Deviations for contrast: secondary and tertiary light text are a step darker than the site's #767676, which is under 4.5 on its own tinted panels.\n\nLeft as is: the sans stack names Inter with system-ui behind it, since the site's own face is not available; mono names JetBrains Mono.\n\nThe test now also pins the look (achromatic neutrals, ink primary, no dark shadows, default radius at the threshold, md below it) and checks one corner pixel that a squircle fills and a circle leaves bare, with the theme's shape on and with smoothing overridden off. Mutating the compiled theme fails it each time, including a mutation that revealed the no-shadow check had only looked at one elevation.","files":null}]}